      "writtenOn": "2026-08-04T17:05:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ls-remote will add a significant amount of latency, on top of having to download a bunch of refs\n```\ntime git ls-remote --quiet sso://chromium/chromium/src.git\n...\ndownloading all refs\n...\ngit ls-remote --quiet sso://chromium/chromium/src.git  7.96s user 32.47s system 71% cpu 56.893 total\n```\n\ngiven that, according to the commit message `remotes stay SHA-1 for the foreseeable future`, and we always fall back to sha1 anyway, the ls-remote step seems to add overhead without any upside. eventually, when GoB supports https://git-scm.com/docs/protocol-v2#_object_format we should use that instead to probe remote since it\u0027s significantlyfaster\n\nfor now, I think it makes sense to return \"sha1\" always, with a comment explaining the situation, and a TODO (linking to the depot_tools git 3.0 support bug) to revisit when remotes start migrating. for reference here\u0027s the GoB tracking object-format work: http://b/534009949",
